      "text_204646949": {
        "jcr:primaryType": "nt:unstructured",
        "jcr:createdBy": "hannah.white@stryker.com",
        "jcr:lastModifiedBy": "hannah.white@stryker.com",
        "jcr:created": "Tue Jun 09 2026 19:07:59 GMT+0000",
        "text": "<p>When exploring areas for improvement, ambulatory surgery centers (ASCs) may benefit from equipment designed with the GI space in mind. Helping reduce room turnover times and the need to dump waste management canisters mid-procedure are just some of the potential benefits.</p>\n<p>Carol Stevens, a nurse with over 35 years of experience in the GI field, recalls a time in her career when she had to manually empty canisters and was splashed.¹ Now there are other options. A constantly closed waste management system like <a href=\"/content/stryker/us/en/surgical-technologies/products/neptune-s.html\">Stryker’s Neptune S</a> helps protect staff from fluid waste exposure.</p>\n<p>“We have to be very concerned as nurses at the end of the procedure and what’s happening with the waste we collected,” she says. “The Neptune has helped because we don’t have to empty a canister that’s sitting on the wall.”</p>\n<p>Stevens, who is a past SGNA president, says the Neptune S system’s integrated specimen collection has also made a positive impact. The device captures polyps that are 2mm in diameter or larger without clogging or needing to turn off suction, and it has a backup basket for added peace of mind.</p>\n",
        "jcr:lastModified": "Tue Jun 09 2026 19:12:29 GMT+0000",
        "sling:resourceType": "stryker/components/content/text",
        "textIsRich": "true",
        "cq:responsive": {
          "jcr:primaryType": "nt:unstructured",
